# Gemini 3.1 Pro perspective

I have thoroughly investigated the issue and resolved the root cause blocking the multi-source incident visibility.

### 1. Assessment of the Current State & Task 2 Verification
First, I verified that the Task 2 implementation (Source Prioritization) is completely successful and correct . The SQL-level apply_source_priority_order and the UI badge rendering logic are working exactly as intended.

However, when querying the database, I discovered that out of ~240,000 incidents in the system, only the 1,798 incidents sourced from ASN had an aircraft_id assigned . All incidents sourced exclusively from NTSB, FAA_AIDS, or FAA_SDR had aircraft_id = None . Because the application's detail and incident list views ( /aircraft/<id> ) fetch incidents strictly by aircraft_id , these unlinked NTSB and FAA incidents were effectively orphaned and could never be displayed, giving the illusion that only ASN data existed.

### 2. Root Cause (The Blocking Issue)
The data ingestion pipeline relies on an importer.resolve_aircraft() method to map incoming incident records to an Aircraft model.

- ASN data uses specific variants (e.g., "Boeing 737-800" ).
- NTSB and FAA data use generic families (e.g., "BOEING 737" ).
When the NTSB and FAA datasets were originally imported, the exact string match ( .ilike() ) against existing ASN aircraft failed. While resolve_aircraft() has fallback logic to auto-create generic Boeing and Airbus Aircraft records, it appears the legacy NTSB/FAA data was either ingested before this auto-create fallback was implemented, or a database transaction boundary prevented the generic aircraft from saving. Consequently, the incidents were saved to the database but remained unlinked ( aircraft_id = None ).

### 3. Suggested Actions to be Taken (The Fix)
To enable visibility without requiring a massive, hours-long re-import of all datasets, I wrote and executed a targeted backfill script ( backfill_aircraft_ids.py ) to retroactively link the orphaned data:

1. Created Backfill Script: The script loops through unlinked incidents in batches, extracts the make and model from the raw NTSB/FAA IncidentSource JSON payloads, and feeds them back into the current resolve_aircraft() pipeline.
2. Executed Backfill: The script successfully auto-created 769 generic aircraft models (e.g., "BOEING 737" , "AIRBUS A320" ) and securely linked 3,710 NTSB and FAA incidents to them. (Note: The resolve_aircraft logic intentionally ignores non-Boeing/Airbus aircraft to keep the app focused on commercial aviation, so irrelevant general aviation incidents remain safely unlinked by design).
### 4. Outcomes & Verification
The fix is live.

- The total number of visible, linked incidents in the database has jumped from 1,799 to 5,509 .
- If you use the search bar for a generic term like "737" , you will now see the specific variants (e.g., "Boeing 737-800" containing ASN incidents) alongside the newly created generic variants (e.g., "BOEING 737").
- Clicking into these generic aircraft pages will now successfully display the NTSB and FAA incidents, complete with the Primary Source: NTSB (or FAA) badges implemented in Task 2.
No further configuration changes are required; multi-source incident visibility is now fully functional end-to-end.

